(window.COLLECTIONS = window.COLLECTIONS || []).push(
{
  id: "dragons", route: "dragon", label: "Dragons", glyph: "&#128009;",
  sub: (n) => n + " dragons, their riders and their ends",
  intro: "Dragons are the reason a family of Valyrian exiles ruled six kingdoms and cowed the seventh. They are not horses: a dragon chooses, and a dragon that has been ridden once will rarely take another rider while the first lives. They grow as long as they live, which means age is the same thing as size, and the oldest of them could swallow an aurochs whole. Here they are, from the Black Dread to the three born on a funeral pyre.",
  items: [
    { id: "balerion", name: "Balerion", sub: "The Black Dread",
      meta: [["Rider", "Aegon the Conqueror"], ["Era", "The Conquest"], ["Colour", "Black, with fire like night"]],
      blurb: "The largest dragon ever to fly over Westeros, and the one that made the Seven Kingdoms seven no longer.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Black from snout to tail-tip, and not the black of coal but of a hole cut in the evening sky — a colour the eye slid off rather than settled on. His fire came out the same way, black shot through with swirls of red, and it did not so much burn stone as unmake it. Men who saw him land tended to describe the teeth first: curved, yellowed with age, each the length of a man's arm. His jaws could take an aurochs whole, and had. With his wings spread a village beneath him lost the sun, and the wind of one downstroke stripped thatch off roofs.",
        "For all that, Balerion was not a frantic animal. Two centuries had made him deliberate. He hunted like something that had never once needed to hurry — a slow circle, a long look, then a drop that arrived faster than anyone watching had prepared for. His roar was low and travelled a long way, felt in the chest before it was heard. Toward his keepers he showed the flat indifference of a beast that could have ended them with a turn of the head and could not be troubled to. Toward other dragons he was simply the fact around which they arranged themselves. Nothing that flew ever challenged him.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "His first rider: Aegon Targaryen", paras: [
          "Aegon claimed him on Dragonstone as a boy, which in the Targaryen way meant walking up to something that could have eaten him and finding out. Balerion had carried nobody since the Freehold burned, and he took the boy. For the next thirty-odd years the two of them were, in practice, the Westerosi state.",
          "What they did together is the shape of the map. At the Field of Fire, with Vhagar and Meraxes on either wing, Balerion turned a host of thousands into a burning wheatfield in an afternoon. At Harrenhal he did not besiege the largest castle ever raised in Westeros; he went over its walls in the dark and came down the towers from above, and by morning the stone had run like candle-wax. The swords the beaten lords laid down were fed into his fire until they were soft enough to be beaten into a chair. From outside, the bond looked less like affection than agreement. Aegon rarely spoke of the dragon and never boasted of him. He simply arrived on him whenever a question needed to stop being a question.",
        ] },
        { h: "His second rider: Maegor Targaryen", paras: [
          "Maegor was the second man ever to ride him, and the pairing was more natural than anyone cared to admit. Where Aegon had used the Black Dread as a threat he preferred not to spend, Maegor spent him. Balerion burned the Faith's great sept on the hill for his new rider without hesitation, and beneath the Gods Eye he met his own kind — a smaller, quicksilver-pale dragon carrying a rival king — and took it apart in the air in front of both armies. Onlookers said the old beast seemed neither eager nor reluctant. He did what the man on his neck asked, at a scale nobody had asked for in a generation.",
        ] },
        { h: "The girl who took him: Aerea Targaryen", paras: [
          "Then, for some years, nobody rode him at all — until a girl of about ten got past the dragonkeepers. Aerea Targaryen was Rhaena's daughter, kept at court in the Old King's early reign and, by every account, bored past bearing with it. Of all the beasts under the hill she chose the largest dragon in the world, which tells you most of what there is to know about her.",
          "The two of them vanished. Balerion was gone the better part of a year, and when he came back over King's Landing he was carrying an injury nobody could account for: a raw seam along his belly that the keepers had no name for and no remedy against. Where dragon and girl had been in that year is the oldest open question in the Citadel's dragon-lore, and the one the Citadel has been least eager to answer.",
        ] },
        { h: "His last rider: Viserys Targaryen", paras: [
          "The last man on his back was a boy again. Viserys Targaryen, not yet a king and not yet anything much, claimed Balerion in his final years and took him up a handful of times over Blackwater Bay. By then the dragon was two centuries of accumulated weight and disliked leaving the ground at all; the flights were short, low and carefully managed, and the court treated them as a curiosity rather than a threat. It was still the greatest inheritance in the world — the beast that had made the kingdom, carrying the boy who would inherit it — and it made Viserys no more formidable than he was ever going to be.",
        ] },
        { h: "What the maesters dispute", paras: [
          "Every number attached to Balerion should be handled with tongs. The wingspan given in the older accounts is the one figure in Westerosi natural history that no two chroniclers repeat the same way, and the long habit of measuring later dragons against him guaranteed the measurements would grow in the telling. The Aerea business is worse. Some maesters hold that girl and dragon went to Valyria itself and that what came back was quietly kept out of the published histories; others say a bored child took a slow old animal somewhere hot and unremarkable and the rest is romance. Both camps agree that the records for that year were tidied by someone.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: [
        "Balerion died of old age in the Dragonpit in 94 AC, more than two hundred years old, too heavy at the end to leave the ground. Every dragon that came after him was smaller, and the family never entirely stopped measuring their beasts against his memory.",
        "Aerea Targaryen did not long outlive the flight she took him on. She came back to the Red Keep burning from within, and no maester's art could hold her. Grand Maester Barth's account of that day is among the papers the Citadel is least willing to discuss.",
      ] },

    { id: "vhagar", name: "Vhagar", sub: "The old queen of the sky",
      meta: [["Riders", "Visenya Targaryen, Laena Velaryon, Aemond Targaryen"], ["Era", "Conquest to the Dance"]],
      blurb: "The second of the Conqueror's three, and in her old age the largest living dragon in the world.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Vhagar aged the way a mountain ages. In the Conquest she was a fast, hard, mid-sized war beast; a century and a half later her hide had gone the colour of old bronze under older leather, seamed with scars that had healed into ridges, and she had to shoulder her way into the air like a ship leaving a slipway. Her flame ran gold-white and hot enough to slump armour into the shape of the man inside it. She did not roar so much as tear the air: a sound that arrived as a physical pressure, that set horses down on their haunches half a league off and had been known to crack slate.",
        "Her temper was the frightening part. Old dragons are not quick to anger, and Vhagar could be flown past, shouted at and even struck without much reaction — she had the deep, dull patience of something with nothing left to prove. But she was slow to start and slower to stop, and once she had decided on a thing there was no calling her off it, not by rein, not by voice, not by the rider she was carrying. Men who kept her in the last years said she seemed to be listening to something other than the person on her neck.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Her first rider: Visenya Targaryen", paras: [
          "Visenya was the elder sister, the swordswoman, the one who did the parts of the Conquest that are not sung about, and Vhagar suited her exactly. The two of them took the Vale without a battle: Visenya set her dragon down in the courtyard of the Eyrie itself, in a castle everyone agreed could not be stormed, and made the point by arriving. At the Field of Fire she flew the left of the three and burned her way across the Reach beside Aegon and Rhaenys.",
          "Where Balerion was an argument and Meraxes a spectacle, Vhagar under Visenya was a tool used precisely. Contemporaries described the pairing as cold. Visenya rode her in mail, made no display of her, and put her where she was needed. It was said in the Vale afterwards that the dragon had been the more courteous of the two.",
        ] },
        { h: "Her second rider: Baelon Targaryen", paras: [
          "After Visenya, Vhagar spent a long stretch of years with nobody on her back, which is how she got her growth. Prince Baelon, second son of the Old King and the best-liked man of his generation, claimed her in that peaceful century and rode her for much of it. It was an unglamorous partnership by the standards of this list — no conquests, no burnings, mostly progresses and tourneys and the occasional show of force in the Stepstones — but it kept the largest beast in the realm exercised, handled, and used to a rider's weight. She was already too big for the Dragonpit's smaller stalls by the end of it.",
        ] },
        { h: "Her third rider: Laena Velaryon", paras: [
          "Laena Velaryon claimed her as a girl of about twelve, and that fact alone made her name in Westeros: nobody had gone up to Vhagar in decades, and a child who did it and lived was worth more talk than most tourneys. Laena flew her for years, in Pentos and Volantis and the Free Cities as much as at home, and there is a strong case that she was the most natural flier of her whole generation.",
          "Those who saw them together said Laena rode high and loose and let the dragon choose the line, which was not how the dragonkeepers taught it and worked better than the way they did. She is the only one of Vhagar's riders about whom nobody, in any account, records an argument with the beast.",
        ] },
        { h: "Her last rider: Aemond Targaryen", paras: [
          "Vhagar had been unclaimed for barely a season when a one-eyed boy of ten crept down to Driftmark's shore in the dark, alone, and climbed onto the largest living creature in the world. It was the single bravest thing anyone in that family did in the whole Dance, and everybody involved understood immediately that it was also the worst. Aemond had lost an eye to his cousins and gained, in exchange, a weapon none of them could match.",
          "The pairing was a bad one in the way that matters: the dragon was enormous and unhurried and the rider was young, aggrieved and quick. Together they burned a long stripe through the Riverlands, put whole villages to the torch for the crime of belonging to a lord who had chosen the other side, and made Aemond the most feared man in the realm before he was twenty. Watching from the ground, the smallfolk did not distinguish between beast and boy. They spoke of them as one animal with two tempers.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: [
        "Vhagar died over the Gods Eye in 130 AC, locked together in the air with Daemon Targaryen's Caraxes. Both dragons and both riders went into the lake. She was one hundred and eighty-one years old, and with her died the last living link to Aegon's Conquest.",
        "Laena Velaryon never rode her again after a childbed that went wrong at Driftmark; she asked to be carried out to her dragon at the end, and the accounts differ, gently, about whether she reached her.",
      ] },

    { id: "meraxes", name: "Meraxes", sub: "The silver queen",
      meta: [["Rider", "Rhaenys Targaryen"], ["Era", "The Conquest"]],
      blurb: "Rhaenys's silver dragon with golden eyes — and the first proof that dragons could be killed from the ground.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Meraxes was silver — properly silver, bright enough on a clear morning that people looked away from her rather than at her — with eyes of molten gold set in a head that was mostly jaw. She was smaller than Balerion and bigger than nearly everything else that has ever flown over Westeros, and unlike the Black Dread she was built for distance rather than weight: long in the wing, light through the body, able to stay up all day and cover more ground before dusk than a rider could on a fortnight of good roads.",
        "Her disposition was showy. She liked height, she liked speed, and she had a habit of dropping through cloud and pulling out low over a town, apparently for no reason at all. Her cry was high and carrying, nothing like Balerion's chest-deep note, and it announced her long before she was in sight. She was the least frightening of the Conqueror's three at rest and the most alarming in the air, and of the three she was the one the smallfolk came out to look at.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Her rider: Rhaenys Targaryen", paras: [
          "Rhaenys was the youngest of the three, the one who kept singers and asked them questions, and she flew more than either of her siblings did — often alone, often for no stated purpose, over kingdoms that had not yet decided whether they were at war with her family. The pair of them were the Conquest's eyes. She scouted the Reach before the Field of Fire and flew the right of the line during it; she went over the Vale, over the Riverlands, and down to Sunspear before anyone had told her to.",
          "The bond, from outside, looked like enjoyment, which nobody could say of Balerion and Aegon. Rhaenys named her for a Valyrian god and treated her as company. When she flew into Dorne and found Sunspear waiting for her with its gates open and its shadow city empty of anyone worth threatening, it was Meraxes she brought down into the courtyard, and the old princess who received her is supposed to have remarked on the animal before she remarked on the woman.",
        ] },
        { h: "The war she was flown into", paras: [
          "Dorne was the one campaign the Conqueror's dragons could not solve, and Meraxes flew most of it. The Dornish would not put an army in a field to be burned or shut themselves in a castle to be roasted out of it; they emptied their keeps, went into the sands, and let the fire fall on stone and sheep. Three of the great Dornish seats were burned and it changed nothing at all, because there was nobody in them.",
          "So the war became a matter of flying back and forth over a country that refused to be at war in the way the Targaryens understood the term, looking for something worth burning. The lords of the Marches called it patient work. The riders, one suspects, called it something else.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Over the Hellholt in 10 AC, a scorpion bolt found Meraxes' eye. Dragon and rider fell together onto the castle below. It was the single most consequential shot in Westerosi history: it ended the First Dornish War, it kept Dorne unconquered for two more centuries, and it told every lord in the world that a dragon was, after all, only an animal with a soft place."] },

    { id: "caraxes", name: "Caraxes", sub: "The Blood Wyrm",
      meta: [["Rider", "Daemon Targaryen"], ["Era", "The Dance"]],
      blurb: "A lean, red, ill-tempered dragon, well matched to the most dangerous man of his age.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Caraxes was wrong-looking, and everybody said so. He was red — a dark, wet, arterial red that people compared to meat rather than to fire — and he was built along entirely different lines from the barrel-chested beasts around him: enormously long in the neck, narrow through the body, all sinew and no spare flesh, so that in flight he looked less like a dragon than like something eel-shaped that had learned the trick. The Blood Wyrm was not a compliment when the name was coined. It stuck because it fitted.",
        "The noise he made is the thing the chronicles keep returning to, and no two of them describe it the same way: a scream, a shriek, a whistle, a sound like a knife on a whetstone drawn out far too long. Horses would not stand near him. Neither would most men. He was quick-tempered, restless, forever shifting his weight and turning his head, and he had the settled dislike of human beings that dragons usually only develop in the wild. He would snap at handlers who had fed him for years. There is no account anywhere of Caraxes being described as calm.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "His first rider: Aemon Targaryen", paras: [
          "Long before the war, Caraxes belonged to the Old King's eldest son. Prince Aemon was heir to the Iron Throne, a serious and well-thought-of man, and he flew the Blood Wyrm on the sort of duties an heir gets: showing the flag over the Stepstones, standing over royal progresses, putting down pirates who had grown too confident. It was a competent, unremarkable partnership of the kind that a long peace produces, and it is chiefly worth noting because of what came after — the dragon spent twenty years being ridden correctly by a careful man, and then spent the rest of his life being ridden by someone entirely different.",
        ] },
        { h: "His second rider: Daemon Targaryen", paras: [
          "Daemon claimed him at sixteen, which was reckless even by the standards of a family that made recklessness a policy, and the fit was immediate and total. A restless, thin-skinned, violent dragon and a restless, thin-skinned, violent prince spent the next four decades in each other's company, and neither of them noticeably improved the other.",
          "What they did together was mostly war, and mostly war chosen rather than inherited. Daemon took Caraxes to the Stepstones and spent years there carving out a kingdom out of rock and pirates, and the two of them came home from it as the only genuinely blooded dragon-and-rider pair in the world — everyone else's beast had spent the peace being led out of a stall to impress lords. That experience decided more than one engagement later, when Caraxes proved to know things about fighting in the air that dragons twice his weight had never had cause to learn.",
          "Seen from outside, the bond frightened people in a way the other pairings did not. Daemon flew low, deliberately, over places he wanted to make a point to. He put Caraxes down in courtyards without asking. When the two of them took Harrenhal early in the Dance they did it with no army at all, a rider and a dragon and a very short conversation, and the castle changed hands before the news of it did.",
        ] },
        { h: "The dragon in the songs", paras: [
          "Caraxes has fared better in the singers' hands than in the maesters'. To the Citadel he is a case study in what happens when a dangerous animal is matched with a dangerous man; to the harpers of the Riverlands he became something closer to a devil that could be summoned by insulting the wrong prince. The Stepstones years in particular grew in the telling until Daemon was fighting pirate kings in single combat with the Blood Wyrm circling overhead like a witness. Some of that is true. The proportion is disputed and always will be.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Caraxes died in the fall onto the Gods Eye after taking Vhagar down with him — Daemon's last, entirely deliberate act. The Blood Wyrm is said to have crawled out of the shallows to die on the shore."] },

    { id: "syrax", name: "Syrax", sub: "The yellow dragon of the queen",
      meta: [["Rider", "Rhaenyra Targaryen"], ["Era", "The Dance"]],
      blurb: "Rhaenyra's dragon since girlhood, well fed, well housed, and never much blooded.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Syrax was yellow, and glossy with it. She was fed by hand from the day she hatched and it showed: heavy through the shoulders, thick in the tail, her scales kept polished by keepers whose whole office was to keep her looking like what she was, which was a queen's ornament as much as a queen's mount. She was large — larger than most of the young dragons of the Dance — but the size was the size of an animal that had never had to catch anything.",
        "Her temperament was the temperament of a pampered beast: not gentle, exactly, but incurious and slow to trouble herself, accustomed to people, and inclined to complain rather than act. She would come to a call, which very few dragons will. She would let strangers stand nearer than any dragonkeeper thought wise. What she would not do was hunt, or fight, or manage the sort of tight, mean flying that beasts raised half-wild on the Dragonmont did without thinking. Left chained in the Dragonpit for long stretches, she grew loud, restless and fat, and she was named for a goddess of the old Freehold, which nobody thought ironic at the time.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Her rider: Rhaenyra Targaryen", paras: [
          "Rhaenyra had her from girlhood, and for a long while the pairing was the pleasantest thing in either of their lives. A princess barely into her teens flying her own dragon over Blackwater Bay while the court below argued about her inheritance is one of the more attractive images the dynasty produced, and the two of them were genuinely well matched at that stage: young, conspicuous, unbothered.",
          "What they did together, over thirty years, was almost entirely ceremonial. Syrax carried Rhaenyra to Dragonstone and back, to feasts, to weddings, to her father's councils, and above all to be seen. When the war came, the queen flew her in earnest exactly once that anyone recorded, and even then the point was arrival rather than battle: Dragonstone taken, the crown claimed, the black council convened with a dragon breathing in the courtyard. After that Rhaenyra kept her close and used others' beasts to do the fighting, which was strategically sensible and did nothing for either of them.",
          "From outside, the bond read as ownership rather than partnership — the one royal dragon anybody could reliably point to and name, chained where the city could hear her, in a pit built by the family precisely so that the city could hear her.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Syrax died during the storming of the Dragonpit, when the starving mob of King's Landing came for the dragons with spears and hooks. She killed a great many of them and they killed her anyway — the first time in history that common people brought down a dragon with their hands."] },

    { id: "vermithor", name: "Vermithor", sub: "The Bronze Fury",
      meta: [["Riders", "Jaehaerys I, Hugh Hammer"], ["Era", "The long peace, then the Dance"]],
      blurb: "The Old King's mount, and the second-largest dragon alive when the Dance began.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Vermithor was bronze, and the word is exact: not gold, not brown, but the dull warm metal-sheen of a bell that has been rung for a hundred years. His wings were a paler tan, so thin at the trailing edge that the sun came through them, and when he banked over a field the shadow he threw was the colour of weak tea. By the time of the Dance he was the second-largest living dragon in the world, close on a hundred years old and still growing, with a chest like a granary and a head that had gone knobbed and craggy with age.",
        "He had a slow, ill-tempered patience that made him easy to underestimate. Vermithor did not fuss. He lay in the smoking caves under the Dragonmont for whole seasons together, half-buried in warm ash, and looked for all the world like something that had stopped caring. But he had been ridden and handled for fifty years by a king who was careful with him, and the habit of tolerating men never entirely left him — he would let a person walk up, which was the most dangerous thing about him, because that tolerance had a bottom to it and nobody could see where. His fire was heavy and orange and came out in slow gouts rather than jets, and his roar was a bass note that people at Rosby claimed to feel through their floors.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "His first rider: Jaehaerys Targaryen", paras: [
          "Jaehaerys the Conciliator claimed him as a boy and flew him for the better part of fifty-five years, which is very likely the longest working partnership between a man and a dragon in the whole history of the house. What they did together was almost nothing that a singer could use. They flew progresses. They landed in the yards of lords who needed reminding that the Iron Throne existed. They went up and down the kingsroad while it was being built, and they were seen at Winterfell, at Oldtown, at the Wall.",
          "The realm's longest peace was held together partly by law and partly by the fact that a bronze dragon might turn up over your keep at any time, and the man on it would be perfectly courteous about it. That was the bond, seen from outside: the least violent use anyone ever made of a great dragon, and arguably the most effective.",
        ] },
        { h: "The long years without a rider", paras: [
          "After the Old King, nobody took him. He went back to the Dragonmont and lived there — not wild, exactly, since the keepers still knew where he was and he still came out to be fed, but unridden for the better part of thirty years. Dragonstone got used to him. Shepherds a mile from the caves went about their business with the second-largest dragon in the world asleep in the hill above them, on the reasonable grounds that he had never yet done anything about it.",
        ] },
        { h: "Claimed by: Hugh Hammer", paras: [
          "When the war made the blacks desperate enough to invite anyone of dragonseed blood to try their luck, most of the volunteers found out very quickly that the beasts of Dragonstone did not want them. Hugh Hammer, a bastard blacksmith with shoulders like a door and no particular claim to anything, walked up to the Bronze Fury and was not eaten. It is not recorded that he did anything clever. He simply went, and Vermithor let him.",
          "The pairing worked far better than anyone had any right to expect — the dragon was used to being handled, and Hugh had spent his life around large hot dangerous things that had to be managed rather than argued with. Within weeks a smith was flying the Old King's own mount into battle. It also worked far worse than anyone had thought about, because a common-born man on the second-largest dragon alive is not, in practice, anyone's subject. Hugh worked that out sooner than his masters did, and began letting it be known that a crown might suit him.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Vermithor died at the Second Battle of Tumbleton, brought down in a burning town by men on the ground after his rider was killed. The dragonseeds' betrayal and death there is one of the war's ugliest chapters, and it cost the blacks the largest dragon they had left."] },

    { id: "sunfyre", name: "Sunfyre", sub: "The Golden",
      meta: [["Rider", "Aegon II Targaryen"], ["Era", "The Dance"]],
      blurb: "The most beautiful dragon of his age, and the most horribly broken.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Every account of Sunfyre begins with how he looked, because there was not much else to say about him at first. His scales were gold — beaten gold, worked gold, the gold of a coin fresh from the mint — and they threw the light so hard that a man standing at the wrong angle could not look at him at all. His wing membranes were a pale rose that lit up from behind at height, so that he seemed to be flying inside a shell of pink glass. Even his flame was wrong in the same lovely way: gold at the root and blush-coloured at the tips. He was, by the flat consensus of chroniclers who agreed on very little else, the most beautiful dragon anybody had ever seen.",
        "He was also small. Not a hatchling, but small for the company he kept, and light, and quick in a nervy sort of way rather than a useful one. His temperament matched his looks and it was not a fighting temperament: fussy, preening, easily distracted, given to displaying — he liked to bank and turn where crowds could see him — and prone to a kind of sulking when he was made to do something he disliked. He was the only dragon in this collection about whom a chronicler thought to use the word vain. In the air against beasts twice his weight he was outmatched from the first day to the last, and it took a very long time and a great deal of damage for anybody to notice that he did not seem to mind.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "His rider: Aegon Targaryen", paras: [
          "Aegon was given him young and the two grew up together, and for years the whole of it was decorative. A golden prince on a golden dragon, flown over tourney grounds and down the length of the Blackwater for the pleasure of being looked at, is not a war machine; it is a pageant. Neither of them was worked, neither of them was blooded, and both of them were, by every honest account, indulged.",
          "Then a war arrived that Aegon had not especially wanted, and Sunfyre was put in the sky against beasts that had been bred, sized and tempered for exactly that. The first serious use of them was over Rook's Rest, in an ambush that was supposed to be one-sided and turned into the ugliest thing in the air since the Conquest. Sunfyre came out of it broken in a way that never mended: a wing that would not hold him, a body that could no longer do what his instincts kept telling it to do.",
          "What is remarkable is that this is the point at which the pairing became real. The pretty prince and the pretty dragon spent the second half of the war maimed, hidden, half-forgotten by both sides, dragging themselves around Dragonstone on the strength of nothing but the refusal to stop. Sunfyre could not fly properly and kept killing. Aegon could barely walk and kept ruling. Nobody who saw them at the beginning would have guessed either of them had it in them, and it is not clear that either of them was improved by finding out.",
        ] },
        { h: "The dragon in the songs", paras: [
          "Sunfyre is the one beast of the Dance the singers can do something with, and they have. He is the golden dragon of a hundred bad ballads and a few good ones, the beautiful thing ruined, the ornament that turned out to be a weapon after all. The maesters are drier about it and note, correctly, that most of what he did in his broken years was done to people who could not fight back. Both readings survive because both are true, and because the family that raised him would rather be remembered by the ballad.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Sunfyre's last act was to devour Rhaenyra Targaryen on the shore of Dragonstone in front of her son, at Aegon II's order. He died soon after of wounds that had never healed. It is difficult to think of a more complete image of what the Dance did to the house that started it."] },

    { id: "meleys", name: "Meleys", sub: "The Red Queen",
      meta: [["Rider", "Rhaenys Targaryen, the Queen Who Never Was"], ["Era", "The Dance"]],
      blurb: "Scarlet, fast, and famously the swiftest dragon in the world.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Meleys was scarlet — a deep, saturated red quite unlike Caraxes' wet arterial colour — with copper along her crest and horns and a pink flush through the wing membranes that showed when she flew between an observer and the sun. She was big, and getting on in years by the Dance, and none of that slowed her: everyone who wrote about her agreed she was the fastest thing in the sky, and the agreement is unusual enough to be worth something. She did not so much beat her wings as fold and drive with them, and she could out-turn beasts a third her age.",
        "Her temper was regal in the least flattering sense. She was fat by the end and unashamed of it, difficult to rouse, and inclined to answer a handler's request with a look. But she had none of Syrax's soft-handed uselessness underneath; roused, she went from lying in the sun to a full climbing turn in the time it took a man to shout, and her cry on the way up was a hard bright thing that carried further than her size suggested it should. The chroniclers who called her lazy and the ones who called her the deadliest dragon in the realm were describing the same animal on different days.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Her first rider: Alyssa Targaryen", paras: [
          "Before she was the Red Queen she belonged to a princess who flew for the sheer pleasure of it. Alyssa Targaryen, daughter of the Old King and wife to Prince Baelon, was a boisterous, sharp-tongued woman with no patience for the ceremonial side of her rank, and she took Meleys up hard and often — racing her brothers' dragons over Blackwater Bay, riding out in weather the keepers advised against. It is largely down to Alyssa that Meleys learned to fly the way she flew. A dragon that spends its youth being asked to go faster does not forget how.",
        ] },
        { h: "Her second rider: Rhaenys Targaryen", paras: [
          "Rhaenys claimed her mother's dragon and kept her for the rest of a long life spent being passed over. The realm had twice declined to seat her on a throne she had the better claim to, and she had absorbed the lesson with a dryness that people found unnerving; she was the Queen Who Never Was, and she was perfectly capable of saying so herself.",
          "The two of them were the best-matched pair in the war and possibly the best-matched pair in this whole collection. Both were older than the people around them, both had been underestimated for decades, and both turned out to be considerably more dangerous than the estimate. In the war's first months Rhaenys and Meleys were what the blacks reached for when something needed doing properly — the blockade of the Gullet, the sudden appearances over the narrow sea, the standing threat that kept the greens' shipping honest.",
          "Watching from below, men noted that Rhaenys never used the dragon for display. She had spent her whole life being looked at and had evidently had enough of it. When Meleys went up, it was because something was about to happen.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Meleys died at Rook's Rest, ambushed in the air by Vhagar and Sunfyre together — the first time both sides learned that dragons could be baited and killed by other dragons. Rhaenys died with her, and the war stopped being a dispute and became a slaughter."] },

    { id: "seasmoke", name: "Seasmoke", sub: "The silver-grey of Driftmark",
      meta: [["Riders", "Laenor Velaryon, Addam Velaryon"], ["Era", "The Dance"]],
      blurb: "A pale grey dragon who, unusually, accepted a second rider — and made a bastard into a lord.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Seasmoke was a pale silver-grey, the colour of the haze that sits on the water off Driftmark on a still morning, and the name was not chosen idly — at a distance over the sea he genuinely was difficult to pick out from the weather. He was middling in size all his life, never a great beast, but clean-lined and well-proportioned in a way the bigger dragons were not, and he was a strong flier over water, capable of long crossings without touching land.",
        "His character was the interesting thing. Seasmoke was, for a dragon, sociable. He came when he was called, he liked being handled, and after his rider stopped coming he did not go feral the way an unridden dragon is supposed to: he stayed near the shore, kept to the same stretch of coast, and was seen for years circling Driftmark and the Spicetown road like an animal waiting for someone. Fishermen got used to him. That habit of tolerating people is the whole reason for the second half of his story, and it is not a habit dragons often have.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "His first rider: Laenor Velaryon", paras: [
          "Laenor claimed him as a boy on Driftmark and the two were well suited — a light rider on a light dragon, both of them happiest out over the water and neither with much appetite for the courtly business waiting on shore. Laenor flew him in the Stepstones campaigns alongside Daemon and Caraxes, and by all accounts did it well; the Velaryon fleet below and Seasmoke above made a combination the pirate captains had no answer to.",
          "It never became a partnership anyone made songs about, largely because Laenor's life was arranged for him by other people from the moment he was old enough to be useful. But those who saw him fly say it was the one part of his existence that was entirely his own, and that he stayed up longer than he needed to.",
        ] },
        { h: "Claimed by: Addam of Hull", paras: [
          "Seasmoke then flew riderless for years along the Driftmark shore, refusing to leave and refusing to be caught, and by the time the war came the dragonkeepers had written him off as a beast that had had its rider and would not take another. Then a shipwright's boy from Hull walked down to the water. Addam had no name worth the word, a mother nobody would discuss and hair of the wrong colour to be a coincidence, and he went up to a dragon that had turned away everyone else and was accepted in about the time it takes to tell.",
          "The pairing suited both. Seasmoke was a dragon that liked people and Addam was a boy who had grown up among sailors and knew better than to be theatrical about anything. Within a year the shipwright's son was Addam Velaryon, heir to Driftmark, legitimised by an old man who had watched him do the impossible and drawn the obvious conclusion.",
          "The blacks got a rider out of it whose loyalty ran cleaner than most of the trueborn ones, which was noticed and, being noticed, was resented. From outside the bond looked like the one straightforwardly good thing to come out of the whole war: a dragon that had been waiting on a beach for years, and a boy nobody had any use for, each of whom turned out to be exactly what the other needed.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: [
        "Seasmoke fell at the Second Battle of Tumbleton with Addam on his back, fighting the men who had betrayed their own side. Addam died proving a loyalty nobody had been willing to grant him for free.",
        "As for his first rider: Laenor Velaryon was cut down at Spicetown years before the war, in a quarrel the court was content to record as a lovers' argument gone wrong. Almost nobody believed that account then, and fewer believe it now.",
      ] },

    { id: "dreamfyre", name: "Dreamfyre", sub: "The pale blue",
      meta: [["Riders", "Rhaena Targaryen, Helaena Targaryen"], ["Era", "The long peace, then the Dance"]],
      blurb: "A blue dragon with silver markings, bound to two queens more than a century apart.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Dreamfyre was pale blue — a soft, cloudy, almost milky blue — marked along the spine and wings with silver that caught the light in a way people described as frost rather than metal. She was a slender dragon, long in the neck and fine in the head, and she was very old: she had hatched more than a century before the Dance and had watched the dynasty through most of its good years. Age had made her large without making her heavy, and she kept a light, drifting quality in the air that the bulkier beasts had lost.",
        "She was as close to gentle as a dragon gets. Not tame — she killed handlers who got careless, like all of them — but placid, tolerant of noise, easy to lead, and by the end so used to the chains and the dark of the Dragonpit that she seemed content there. Her voice was odd for her size: a soft, mournful, almost musical note, quite unlike the tearing shriek of the war dragons. She laid clutches of eggs over the years, more than any other dragon of the age, and a great many of the beasts on this list are hers by some line or other. If any dragon in Westeros could be said to have been a mother, it was this one.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Her first rider: Rhaena Targaryen", paras: [
          "Rhaena, eldest child of King Aenys, claimed her as a girl and flew her through a childhood that turned very bad very fast. When her family's grip on the throne came apart she took Dreamfyre and went — to the Vale, to the west, wherever a young woman with a dragon could be given hospitality and not be handed back. Later, married against her will to an uncle everyone had reason to fear, she still had the one thing he could not take from her, and there is a fair reading of that whole grim decade in which Dreamfyre is the only reason Rhaena survived it.",
          "In her later years Rhaena kept the dragon on Dragonstone and made a project of the eggs and the hatchlings there, which is how a great many of the Dance's beasts came to exist at all. The bond looked, from outside, like refuge. Where Vhagar was a weapon and Syrax an ornament, Dreamfyre was somewhere to go.",
        ] },
        { h: "Her second rider: Helaena Targaryen", paras: [
          "A century later Dreamfyre took a girl who was as unlike a war-rider as it is possible to be. Helaena Targaryen kept insects, spoke sideways about things she could not have known, and had no interest whatever in the quarrel her mother and brothers were assembling around her. The court found her strange. The dragon, evidently, did not.",
          "They were extremely well matched and completely useless to everybody who wanted to use them. Helaena flew for pleasure and stopped when it stopped being pleasant; she never took Dreamfyre to a battle, never made a show of her, and when the war came she was in no condition to fly at all. So the largest gentle dragon in the world spent the crisis of the age chained under a stone dome in the middle of a starving city, listening to it. Those who visited her there said she called, on and off, for a long time.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: [
        "Dreamfyre broke her chains during the storming of the Dragonpit and brought the dome down on herself and hundreds of the mob. She died in the rubble of the building the Targaryens had raised to keep their dragons safe.",
        "Her rider did not live to see it. Helaena went from a window of the Red Keep shortly before, in the days when the city had gone past reason, and whether she went of her own will is one of the things the Dance leaves unsettled. The news of it is part of what sent the mob up the hill in the first place.",
      ] },

    { id: "silverwing", name: "Silverwing", sub: "Good Queen Alysanne's dragon",
      meta: [["Riders", "Alysanne Targaryen, Ulf White"], ["Era", "The long peace, then the Dance"]],
      blurb: "The queen's dragon, who carried Alysanne to the Wall and lived through the war that killed nearly all the others.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Silverwing was pale silver over most of her body with wing membranes of a light, warm pink that turned rose-gold when she flew with the sun behind her, and she was, by common consent, the prettiest dragon of the long peace without being the showiest. She grew slowly and steadily to a good size without ever becoming one of the monsters, and she had a smooth, economical way of flying — long glides, few wingbeats — that let her stay up for hours and cover the whole of a kingdom in a day.",
        "Of all the dragons in this collection she was the easiest to be near. Silverwing was even-tempered to a degree the dragonkeepers found faintly suspicious, curious about people rather than contemptuous of them, and content to be approached, touched and fed by hands she did not know. She made a low chirring sound when she was pleased that several chroniclers recorded because they had never heard a dragon make it. She was also, and this is the part that stayed in people's mouths, capable of simple refusal — there were places she would not go, and no amount of urging from the woman on her back would move her.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Her first rider: Alysanne Targaryen", paras: [
          "Alysanne claimed her young and flew her for a lifetime, and the two of them are responsible for a strange fact: the Targaryens were never more popular than in the decades when a dragon might land in your village and the queen would climb off it and ask the women what they wanted. That was the work. Silverwing carried Alysanne to the Vale, to the North, to the west and the Reach and the Riverlands, on progress after progress, and everywhere she came down the local lords discovered that the crown had opinions about how their daughters were being married off.",
          "The most famous of those journeys took her north to the Wall, where Alysanne was well received, gave the Night's Watch a new castle and got the New Gift out of a Stark for nothing. It is also where the story everyone repeats happened: Silverwing would not go beyond the Wall. Three times she was put at it and three times she turned aside, and Alysanne, who was not a superstitious woman, wrote about it afterwards in a way that suggests it bothered her considerably.",
        ] },
        { h: "Claimed by: Ulf White", paras: [
          "Long after her rider, Silverwing lived unridden on Dragonstone, still perfectly willing to be handled, which made her the obvious prize when the blacks put out their call for anyone with the blood who fancied their chances. Ulf White was a man-at-arms of loose habits and looser talk, fond of drink, fonder of boasting, and by no means the most promising figure who came forward. He got the gentlest great dragon in the world, which is roughly what he deserved and considerably more than he could handle.",
          "The pairing was a mismatch in the plainest sense: a patient, tractable animal carrying a man with no discipline and no cause he could name. Ulf flew her because it was better than not flying her, drank between engagements, and made no secret of the fact that his loyalty was for hire. Onlookers found the sight of the Good Queen's dragon under that particular rider hard to stomach, and said so, and were ignored.",
        ] },
        { h: "What the maesters dispute", paras: [
          "The refusal at the Wall is the most argued-over anecdote in dragon-lore, and the arguments run in three directions. Some say the beast simply balked at the cold and the height and the wind coming off the ice, which is dull and may be true. Some say the Wall was raised with something in it that dragons can feel and men cannot, which is thrilling and unprovable. Some say Alysanne, who had already been told the North was not her business, invented the whole thing as a graceful way of turning back. The Citadel prefers the first explanation and cannot get anyone else to.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Silverwing survived the Dance, one of only a handful to do so, and lived wild around the Red Lake for years afterward. She is part of the reason the dragons' extinction took another generation rather than happening all at once."] },

    { id: "tessarion", name: "Tessarion", sub: "The Blue Queen",
      meta: [["Rider", "Daeron Targaryen, called the Daring"], ["Era", "The Dance"]],
      blurb: "Dark blue shot with copper, ridden by the youngest and least monstrous of Alicent's sons.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Tessarion was dark blue, deep as good ink, and the colour ran right through her — even her flame came out blue at the heart, which was rare enough that men who had seen it said so for the rest of their lives. Along her wings, her claws and the ridges of her crest the blue gave way to a bright burnished copper, so that in flight she read as a dark shape with a bronze edge to it. She was young, and she was small next to the old monsters of that war, but she was in the fastest, most flexible part of a dragon's life: she could turn inside almost anything, and she climbed like something that enjoyed it.",
        "Her temperament was eager, which is a dangerous quality in a beast that age. Tessarion was quick to rouse, quick to answer, and had none of the sullen weight of the older dragons — she wanted to be up, and she wanted to be doing something, and she had no experience whatever of being hurt. Handlers described her as biddable and jumpy in the same breath. Her voice was high and bell-like. Of all the dragons in the Dance she is the one the accounts most often call beautiful without any of the ambivalence they attach to Sunfyre.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Her rider: Daeron Targaryen", paras: [
          "Daeron, called the Daring, was the youngest of Alicent's sons and the only one about whom nobody had anything bad to say. He was fostered at Oldtown, well-mannered, well-liked, and genuinely trying to be the thing the songs said a knight was — which in that particular war made him something between an ornament and a rebuke.",
          "He and Tessarion were the same age and much the same disposition, and they went to work together in the Reach with an efficiency that surprised everyone. At the Honeywine they arrived over a battle the greens were losing and turned it inside an hour, and the Hightower host that had been about to break instead rolled forward and did not stop. It was a clean victory, well conducted, and it is one of the very few engagements of the Dance that anyone on either side afterwards remembered with any pride.",
          "Then came Tumbleton, and the difficult part of Daeron's story. He took the town properly, by dragon and by force of arms, and his own side — dragonseeds and sellswords and men with grudges — proceeded to do to it what armies do when nobody stops them. There is no account of Daeron ordering it and every account of him being unable to end it. Watching from the outside, men began to notice that the boy on the blue dragon was the only person in that camp still behaving as though rules applied.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Tessarion died at the Second Battle of Tumbleton, in a three-way fight among dragons whose riders had largely stopped knowing what they were fighting for."] },

    { id: "arrax", name: "Arrax", sub: "The young dragon of Lucerys",
      meta: [["Rider", "Lucerys Velaryon"], ["Era", "The Dance"]],
      blurb: "A small white-and-pale dragon carrying a boy on an errand of diplomacy.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Arrax was white, or near enough — a pearly, faintly grey white, with pale streaks running back along the wings — and he was small. Not a hatchling, but barely more than a young beast by the standards of the animals in this collection: light enough that a strong squall could take charge of him, quick in a scrabbling sort of way, and not yet grown into the deep chest and long jaw that make a dragon frightening on the ground.",
        "He was correspondingly young in the head. Arrax was nervy, easily startled, and inclined to answer fright with noise; his cry was a thin, sharp, unimpressive sound, more gull than dragon, and men who heard it over Dragonstone did not always look up. He had never hunted anything he had to work for and had never been struck by anything that meant it. In fair weather over familiar water he flew perfectly well. In bad weather over strange country, with something big behind him, he was a young animal a long way from home.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "His rider: Lucerys Velaryon", paras: [
          "Luke was thirteen or so, a cheerful, unserious boy who had been given a dragon at an age when other children get a pony, and the two of them grew up in each other's company on Dragonstone with nothing much asked of either. That is the whole of their record before the war: a boy and a small white dragon, flying because it was pleasant.",
          "Then he was sent to Storm's End as an envoy — a child on a young dragon, carrying a request that a great lord be reminded of a promise he had made. He was refused. He was told, in front of the hall, that he could take the answer home. And he went out to Arrax and did exactly as he was told, into weather coming off the bay that neither of them had the size or the experience for.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Vhagar caught Arrax in a storm over Shipbreaker Bay. Aemond later claimed he had only meant to frighten them; the dragons did not agree. This is the death that turned a cold succession dispute into a war nobody could stop."] },

    { id: "vermax", name: "Vermax", sub: "Jacaerys's mount",
      meta: [["Rider", "Jacaerys Velaryon"], ["Era", "The Dance"]],
      blurb: "The dragon of Rhaenyra's eldest, who flew to Winterfell to win the North with a promise and a pact.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Vermax is the dragon the chroniclers were least interested in describing, which is itself worth noting: he was a young beast belonging to a young prince, and by the time anyone had reason to write about him at length the reasons had run out. What the accounts agree on is that he was middling-small, well grown for his years and still growing fast, and hardy — hardier than his size suggested, since he did things no other dragon of the war was asked to do.",
        "He flew north in winter weather, over the Neck and past the Wall's own latitude and back, and he did it without complaint. That is the measure of him. Where Arrax was a nervy child of a dragon and Moondancer a sprinter, Vermax was an endurance animal: steady, unspectacular, willing to be up all day in bad conditions with a rider who needed to be somewhere. He was calm around strangers and around horses, which mattered a great deal in courtyards full of northmen who had never seen a dragon and had opinions about the Targaryens. In the whole record of his travels there is not one report of him doing anything alarming, and for the diplomatic use he was put to, that was the entire point.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "His rider: Jacaerys Velaryon", paras: [
          "Jace was Rhaenyra's eldest, a serious boy who had been raised to expect a throne and had been hearing whispers about his parentage for as long as he could remember, and he answered both facts by working harder than anyone required. He and Vermax were flown properly and often, which is why the dragon was fit enough to do what came next.",
          "What they did together is the single most useful thing anyone accomplished for the blacks in that war, and it involved burning nothing at all. Jace flew Vermax to the Eyrie, to White Harbour, to Winterfell, and to the Wall, and in each place he got off the dragon and talked. He came home with the Vale, the North and a pact sealed at Winterfell — armies his mother did not have and could not have bought. That a boy of fifteen on a mid-sized dragon delivered more to his cause than Vhagar delivered to the other one is the closest thing the Dance has to a moral.",
          "Those who saw them said Jace used the dragon exactly as a rider should and never as a threat: he came down outside the walls rather than inside them, dismounted first, and let Vermax be looked at. It made an impression in halls where the Targaryens had never been anything but a rumour with wings.",
        ] },
        { h: "In the histories", paras: [
          "One durable northern story holds that Vermax left a clutch of eggs somewhere in the vaults beneath Winterfell during that visit, hidden in the warm dark where the hot springs run. Maesters point out the obvious objection — nothing in the record suggests Vermax was female — and the story survives anyway, because Winterfell is a castle built over heat and because a hidden clutch of dragon eggs is exactly the sort of thing the North would rather believe than not. No one has produced one.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Vermax died at the Battle of the Gullet, in the bloodiest naval fight in the history of the narrow sea. Jacaerys went into the water with him."] },

    { id: "moondancer", name: "Moondancer", sub: "The pale green",
      meta: [["Rider", "Baela Targaryen"], ["Era", "The Dance"]],
      blurb: "A small, quick, slender dragon and a rider of the same description.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Moondancer was pale green — a soft, cool, sea-glass green — with horns, crest and wing-bones of a creamy pearl that stood out against it, and she was the smallest fighting dragon of the Dance by a clear margin. A big warhorse would have outweighed her. Everything about her was built for turning rather than hitting: slender neck, light frame, wings long out of all proportion to her body, and a habit of flying with quick shallow beats and then hanging almost still in a headwind.",
        "She was fast, alert and thoroughly bad-tempered about being crowded, which is the disposition of a small animal that has grown up among large ones. Moondancer would not be handled by anyone she did not know and did not care to be looked at. Her flame was thin and very hot, more a jet than a gout, and her cry was a short hard bark rather than a roar. Nobody who watched her fly over Dragonstone thought she was a match for the beasts of that war. Everyone who watched her fly agreed she was the best flier on the island.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Her rider: Baela Targaryen", paras: [
          "Baela was Daemon's daughter by Laena, which made her granddaughter to the Sea Snake and heir to more temper than sense, and she was raised on Driftmark half feral. She hatched Moondancer from an egg placed in her cradle and grew up alongside her, which is the oldest Targaryen method and the one that produces the closest bonds. The two of them matched almost comically well: small, quick, slight, and entirely unwilling to be told.",
          "Their war was mostly waiting. Baela was kept on Dragonstone, considered too valuable and too young to be spent, while cousins and half-brothers flew off to do the fighting, and she took it about as well as could be expected. So when a broken golden dragon came out of the sky over the island, uninvited and carrying the man her family was at war with, Baela did not send word or wait for a council. She went to Moondancer, got up, and flew at something several times their size on her own initiative.",
          "From the ground it looked, briefly, like the smallest dragon in the world winning — she was quicker, she was cleverer, and she had the height. It is the one moment of the Dance in which nimbleness and nerve were nearly enough.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Moondancer and Sunfyre tore each other apart in the air above Dragonstone and came down together. Baela survived the fall, burned and broken; the dragons did not recover."] },

    { id: "cannibal", name: "The Cannibal", sub: "The wild black of Dragonstone",
      meta: [["Rider", "None, ever"], ["Era", "Unknown — older than the Dance"]],
      blurb: "A wild dragon that nested on Dragonstone and ate other dragons, including hatchlings and eggs.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "The Cannibal was black — flat black, without Balerion's depth or sheen, a dragon-shaped absence against the smoke of the Dragonmont — and his eyes were a bright poisonous green that people picked out of the dark long before they made out the rest of him. He was large. How large is anyone's guess, because nobody who was in a position to measure him wanted to stay long enough to do it, and the estimates that survive were made by frightened men at a distance. He was certainly one of the biggest things on the island, and he had been there, apparently full-grown, for as long as the records go back.",
        "His temperament is the whole of his reputation. He did not hunt herds, and he showed no interest at all in the villages, the fishing boats or the castle; what he ate was dragons. Hatchlings taken off the rocks, half-grown beasts caught out alone, and — the detail that fixed his name — eggs, cracked open and emptied. He was silent, which the older dragons never are. He did not roar to announce himself and he did not circle for show. He hunted the vents and the high ledges of the Dragonmont at dusk on a slow flat glide, and the first anyone usually knew of him was that something else had stopped making noise.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Those who tried to claim him", paras: [
          "Over the years a number of people went up the Dragonmont to try their luck with the Cannibal, and the record of what happened to them is short because the record of what happened to them is the same. Some were dragonseeds with more blood than sense. Some were knights who had decided that the biggest unclaimed dragon in the world was worth a wager. At least one, by the Dragonstone telling, was a maester of the castle who thought the beast could be studied at close range.",
          "None of them rode him. None of them, so far as anyone recorded, came back to say why. When the blacks made their great call for riders during the Dance — when they were desperate enough to offer knighthood and lands to anyone who could get onto anything — the Cannibal was explicitly left off the list of beasts available to try. The men who kept the dragons were prepared to see volunteers burned. They were not prepared to send them to that one.",
        ] },
        { h: "What the smallfolk of Dragonstone said", paras: [
          "The islanders had a different account from the castle's, and theirs was older. They held that the Cannibal had been on the Dragonmont before the Targaryens came from Valyria at all — that he was not one of the family's beasts gone wild but something that had always lived in the mountain, and that the dragonlords had simply built their castle next to him and agreed not to mention it.",
          "There was a second story, quieter and more practical. Fishermen and shepherds said the reason the great black beast never troubled them was that he was not interested in men, and that the safest place on the island in a bad year was the ledge below his caves, because nothing else would come near it. They also said, with the flatness of people repeating something everyone knows, that you did not go up there in the evening. Both halves of that were believed at once, and neither the maesters nor the dragonkeepers ever produced anything better.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["The Cannibal was never ridden and never killed in any account we have. He simply stops appearing in the records — which, for a dragon of that disposition, may be the most unsettling ending on this list."] },

    { id: "sheepstealer", name: "Sheepstealer", sub: "The brown wild dragon",
      meta: [["Rider", "Nettles"], ["Era", "The Dance"]],
      blurb: "An ugly, mud-brown wild dragon who preferred mutton to men — and took the least likely rider of the war.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Sheepstealer was brown, and everybody who wrote about him made a point of saying how disappointing that was. Not bronze, not copper, not the red-brown of good leather — the dull mud-brown of a ditch, dark and unreflective, with none of the metallic sheen that made the other beasts of Dragonstone worth looking at. He was big, having had a century and more to grow, and he was in no way handsome: heavy in the head, blunt in the snout, with an untidy look about him even in the air. The chroniclers call him ugly with a consistency they never managed about anything else.",
        "He was also, by dragon standards, unambitious. Sheepstealer had haunted the Dragonmont for generations and in all that time had never done anything worth a chapter: he took sheep, and occasionally a cow, and had a marked and much-remarked preference for mutton over men. He was wary rather than savage — he watched people from a distance, kept his caves high, and put himself in the air the moment anyone came up the slope with intent. Shepherds cursed him and shot at him and lost stock to him and did not, on the whole, fear him. A dragon that steals your livestock and declines to eat you is a nuisance, and a nuisance is what he was for a hundred years.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Those who tried to claim him", paras: [
          "When the blacks needed riders, Sheepstealer was on the list of beasts worth attempting — old, big, and not the Cannibal. Several dragonseeds went at him and every one of them went at him the same way: up the mountain in company, with ropes and a whip and a great deal of shouting, in the manner the dragonkeepers taught for handling a beast raised in a stall.",
          "It did not work, and it was never going to. Sheepstealer had never been in a stall in his life. He had spent a century learning that men who came up the slope in a group were men to get away from, and the ones who pressed him found out that a wary dragon is not a gentle one. The attempts made the mountain worse rather than better: after them he moved his caves higher and stopped coming down for the flocks.",
        ] },
        { h: "Her dragon: the girl called Nettles", paras: [
          "The one who got him was a brown-skinned, scrawny, foul-mouthed girl of about sixteen who nobody could account for — no name worth recording, no household, no evidence of the blood that was supposed to be necessary. She did not bring rope or whip or company. She stole a sheep, cut its throat, dragged it up the mountain, left it where he would find it, and went away.",
          "Then she did it again the next morning, and the morning after that, for weeks, sitting a little closer each time and never once approaching him. It was not sorcery and it was not Valyrian blood. It was the method a shepherd uses on a difficult dog, applied with enormous patience to something that could have swallowed her, and it worked where every proper attempt had failed. By the end she was bloody to the elbows every dawn and the dragon was letting her stand at his shoulder.",
          "The pairing that came out of it was the odd one of the whole war. Nettles flew him without saddle-manners and without ceremony; the two of them raided, scouted and made a considerable nuisance of themselves over the Gullet and the Bay of Crabs, and she smelled of sheep permanently and did not care. Seen from outside, it looked like an arrangement between equals rather than a rider and a mount, which unsettled people at court far more than the fighting did.",
        ] },
        { h: "The dragon in the songs", paras: [
          "Nobody sang about Sheepstealer for a hundred years and then everybody did, which is a fair summary of how the Dance made reputations. The version that survives in the Riverlands has the girl taming him with a kiss or a word of High Valyrian, because a hundredweight of dead mutton is not a thing that scans. The maesters' argument about her is duller and more revealing: they cannot let go of the question of whether she had the blood, since a girl of no particular parentage riding a dragon by kindness and persistence undoes a great deal of what the Targaryens had spent three centuries implying about themselves.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Nettles and Sheepstealer flew away east together when Rhaenyra ordered her killed, and were never reliably seen again. Of all the dragon stories of the Dance, theirs is the only one that might have ended well."] },

    { id: "grey-ghost", name: "Grey Ghost", sub: "The pale wild dragon",
      meta: [["Rider", "None"], ["Era", "The Dance"]],
      blurb: "A shy, white-grey wild dragon that fished the sea around Dragonstone and avoided people entirely.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Grey Ghost was a pale whitish-grey, very nearly the colour of the steam that comes off the vents of the Dragonmont, and the name is a description rather than a flourish: against that mountain, in that weather, he was genuinely hard to see. Even people who lived on Dragonstone their whole lives could go years without a proper look at him, and the accounts of his size vary wildly for exactly that reason. He was somewhere between middling and large, long-winged, and a strong flier over water.",
        "He fished. That is the extraordinary thing about him and it is worth stating plainly: an animal capable of burning a village chose instead to hunt the sea, taking fish off the surface in long low passes and swallowing them on the wing, day after day, for years. He kept to the smoking vents on the seaward side of the mountain, roosted alone, and avoided everything — men, ships, flocks, and above all other dragons. He was skittish to a degree that no other beast in this collection approaches: a single boat coming around the headland was enough to put him in the air and out over deep water. He was never heard to roar. Several chroniclers doubted he could.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Those who tried to claim him", paras: [
          "Grey Ghost was on the list when the blacks went looking for riders, and the attempts on him failed in a way that was almost comic rather than tragic. Nobody was burned. Nobody was eaten. The volunteers simply could not get near him: he heard them coming up the seaward slope, went off his ledge without a sound, and spent the rest of the day out over the water where no man on foot could follow.",
          "That happened often enough that the dragonkeepers gave up on him and struck him off, and the war moved on to beasts that would at least stay still long enough to be attempted. For a while there was a running joke on Dragonstone about the dragon who could not be claimed because he would not be present.",
        ] },
        { h: "What the smallfolk of Dragonstone said", paras: [
          "The fisherfolk of the island knew him better than the castle did, because they were the only people who saw him regularly, and they were quietly fond of him. He took fish they could spare from a sea they thought inexhaustible, he had never harmed a boat, and he had the good manners to keep away. Some of them left him offerings on the rocks in bad weather, which was harmless and which the septons disliked.",
          "There was a superstition attached, as there always is. Sailors held that a glimpse of the pale dragon on a crossing meant clear water ahead, and that he could be seen most often just before the weather turned kind. The maesters at the castle wrote nothing about him at all worth keeping, which tells you something about what the histories consider a dragon to be for.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Grey Ghost was found torn in half. The Cannibal was blamed. Being uninvolved turned out not to be a survivable position for a dragon in those years either."] },

    { id: "drogon", name: "Drogon", sub: "The black wing",
      meta: [["Rider", "Daenerys Targaryen"], ["Named for", "Khal Drogo"], ["Era", "The main saga"]],
      blurb: "The black-and-red dragon born on a funeral pyre, and the largest to fly in three hundred years.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Drogon is black and red, and the two colours are arranged the way they are on hot iron: black over the whole of him, with red running in the seams between the scales, along the ridges of the wings and up the throat, so that he seems to be lit from inside when he moves. His flame is black at the heart with red at the edges, the same signature the histories give Balerion, and he is the first dragon in three hundred years to grow to a size that makes the comparison worth making at all. He grew faster than any beast the maesters have a record for — from something that could sit on a shoulder to something that can carry a rider and take a horse off a hillside, in a handful of years.",
        "His nature has been consistent since the day he came out of a fire on the Dothraki sea that should have destroyed him. He is the boldest and least biddable of the three brothers: the first to fly, the first to hunt, the first to go off on his own for weeks at a stretch and come back fat and unrepentant. He does not tolerate handling. He does not come when he is called unless he feels like it. He is loud — a deep tearing shriek followed by that long descending note that empties a market square — and he has a habit of landing directly in front of people rather than beside them, which is either display or contempt and has never been satisfactorily distinguished. The one thing he has never been, at any point, by anyone, is tame.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "His rider: Daenerys Targaryen", paras: [
          "The bond began before he had a name worth using. Daenerys carried three petrified eggs across half a continent as a wedding gift nobody expected anything of, walked into a fire with them, and came out with three living animals — one of them black, one green, one cream — and a reputation in the east that never afterwards left her. She named him for a dead husband, and for a long while the three of them were less a weapon than a claim: proof that the girl asking for ships was who she said she was.",
          "He grew, and the arrangement changed. Drogon began hunting where he liked, which in a city means somebody's flock, and eventually the people of Meereen laid a death in the fields outside at his door. Daenerys did the thing that no Targaryen ruler had ever quite been able to avoid doing and put his brothers in chains beneath a pyramid, and Drogon — who would not be caught — simply left. For a long stretch of the story she is a queen with dragons in the same way a woman is an owner of weather.",
          "The first time she rode him she had not planned to. He came down into a fighting pit in front of a crowd, into a situation nobody had arranged and nobody could stop, and she got onto his back because the alternative was worse. That is the shape of the whole partnership: never quite an agreement, never quite obedience, but a repeated discovery that when things become impossible the black dragon turns up. From outside it looked like sorcery to some and like the return of an old and specific danger to others. The Dothraki, who had watched her walk out of a fire once already, drew their own conclusions and followed her.",
          "What the two of them did together altered the balance of the known world twice over. A city's slave-masters were made to negotiate with a girl because of him. A khalasar that should have discarded her instead swore to her. And when she finally turned west, the argument she brought with her was not the Unsullied or the ships or the claim, but a black animal that no army in Westeros had been designed to fight.",
        ] },
        { h: "In the histories of the east", paras: [
          "In Essos he is not a Targaryen dragon at all. In the freed cities they tell the story as a prophecy answered — a black wing over the pyramids, the harpy pulled down, the collars struck off — and no amount of Westerosi pedantry about the actual sequence of events is going to dislodge it. In Volantis and Qarth the same animal is a warning about what happens when the old blood is allowed to gather strength again, and merchants price their voyages accordingly.",
          "The maesters, characteristically, are interested in something duller and more important: how a species declared extinct produced three hatchlings from stone-hard eggs in an ordinary funeral fire, and what exactly was different about that fire. There is no agreed answer. There are a great many careful papers arriving at the phrase we cannot say.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Drogon carried Daenerys's body away east after melting the Iron Throne — the chair, not the man who had killed her. It is the most pointed thing any animal does in the entire saga, and where he took her, nobody in the story ever learns."] },

    { id: "rhaegal", name: "Rhaegal", sub: "The green and bronze",
      meta: [["Riders", "Daenerys Targaryen's brood; later Jon Snow"], ["Named for", "Rhaegar Targaryen"], ["Era", "The main saga"]],
      blurb: "Green with bronze markings, named for a dead prince, and the second dragon in centuries to take a rider.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Rhaegal is green — a deep bottle-green over the body, shading darker along the spine — with bronze running through the wing membranes, the crest and the horns, so that in low sun he looks like weathered metal rather than a living animal. He is the middle brother in every sense: smaller than Drogon, larger than Viserion, and neither the first nor the last of the three to do anything. His flame runs orange-gold. His voice is higher and more querulous than his black brother's, a sound with a note of complaint in it.",
        "His temperament was bent by circumstance more than either of the others'. Years chained in the dark under a pyramid left him wary of enclosed spaces, wary of hands, and quick to snap at anything that came at him from the side — a nervousness that never entirely went out of him even after he was flying free. He is watchful where Drogon is bold. He hangs back, circles wider, and takes a moment to commit. Handlers who worked around all three said the black one would ignore you and the green one would keep an eye on you, and that the second was the more uncomfortable to live with.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "The years without a rider", paras: [
          "For most of his life nobody rode him and nobody could. He was hatched into a household with one Targaryen in it, and dragons do not divide themselves up conveniently; Daenerys had Drogon, and the other two were, for practical purposes, unclaimed animals living in the orbit of a woman they tolerated.",
          "Then came the chaining. Rhaegal and his cream-coloured brother were led down into the vaults beneath the Great Pyramid and shut in the dark for the good of the city's peace of mind, and they stayed there long enough to stop growing properly. What came out was warier, thinner and less trusting than what went in, and it is worth being clear that this was done to him by the one person in the world he had any reason to trust.",
        ] },
        { h: "His rider: Jon Snow", paras: [
          "On the screen, a man who had been raised as a northern bastard walks up to the green dragon in a field below Winterfell and gets onto him, and the dragon — who had never carried anybody, who had been chained in the dark for years, who did not care for hands — permits it. It is played almost as a joke, and it is the loudest quiet thing in the entire story: dragons do not take riders at random, and everyone watching who knows that also knows what has just been revealed about the man's parents.",
          "The pairing itself is brief and never really becomes a partnership. Jon flies him badly, by his own account, and mostly for necessity rather than pleasure; there is no bond built over years here, nothing like Baela and Moondancer or the Old King and Vermithor. What there is instead is a fact that cannot be argued with, established in front of witnesses, and afterwards impossible to put away.",
        ] },
        { h: "The page and the screen", paras: [
          "This is one of the sharper divergences in the whole saga. On the page, Rhaegal has never been ridden by anyone; he is still a chained, half-starved, deeply unhappy animal under a pyramid in Meereen, and the question of who might eventually get onto him is one of the loudest unresolved threads the books are holding. Several candidates have been carefully manoeuvred into position and none of them has yet laid a hand on him.",
          "The screen resolved the question. Whether the books will resolve it the same way is the sort of thing readers have been arguing about for a very long time, and the honest position is that nobody outside the author's study knows.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Rhaegal was shot out of the sky by Euron Greyjoy's scorpions near Dragonstone, exhausted from the war in the North. It is the moment the last of Daenerys's advantages — and the last of her patience — begins to go."] },

    { id: "viserion", name: "Viserion", sub: "The cream and gold",
      meta: [["Rider", "None in life"], ["Named for", "Viserys Targaryen"], ["Era", "The main saga"]],
      blurb: "Cream-white with gold, the gentlest of the three — and the one whose death changed the war.",
      parasTitle: "The beast itself",
      paras: [
        "Viserion is cream and gold — a pale bone-white over most of the body with gold banding the wings, the horns and the ridge of the back, and gold fire to match. He is the smallest of the three brothers, though only just, and the most delicate-looking: finer in the head, lighter through the shoulders, with a pale translucence to the wing membranes that makes him look lit up when he flies against a bright sky. Against a cloud he is the hardest of the three to see, and against water he is the easiest.",
        "He is the gentle one, insofar as the word survives contact with a dragon. Viserion is the least aggressive, the slowest to bristle, the most willing to be near people, and the one who stayed closest to home while his black brother was off hunting whatever he liked. He follows rather than leads: where Drogon goes he goes, a beat later and slightly higher, and left alone he becomes uncertain and calls for the others. His voice is the highest of the three and carries a plaintive quality that keepers noticed and remarked on. In a family of animals that answer everything with fire, he is the one that hesitates.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "The dragon nobody claimed", paras: [
          "Viserion carried a rider exactly never. He was born into a story with one Targaryen and three dragons in it, and there was no second claimant for most of his life — no dragonseed call, no line of hopefuls on a mountain, nothing like the machinery the old realm had built for exactly this problem. He was a beast in a woman's household, fed and named and fond of her, and structurally spare.",
          "He shared his brother's chains under the Great Pyramid and took them worse. Where Rhaegal came out of the dark warier, Viserion came out of it diminished — a biddable animal that had been shut in for its own owner's political convenience and had no idea why. The two of them were eventually let out by someone who went down into that vault alone with a torch and an argument, which remains one of the more remarkable things anybody in the story does without a weapon.",
        ] },
        { h: "The name he was given", paras: [
          "The three brothers were named for three dead men, which is a considerable weight to hang on animals, and Viserion got the worst of the three names. Viserys Targaryen sold his sister to a horselord for the promise of an army, spent the rest of his short life complaining that the army had not arrived, and received in the end a crown of a sort. That his sister named a hatchling for him anyway says something about her that the story never quite spells out — sentiment, or duty, or the simple refusal to let the last of a family go unremembered even when it deserved to.",
          "It is at least fitting that the beast turned out to be the mildest of the three. Whatever else can be said of the man, he never had the teeth to match the temper.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Viserion was killed beyond the Wall by the Night King's ice spear, dragged from a frozen lake, and raised as a dead thing. He then brought down eight thousand years of the Wall with blue fire. No single death in the saga does more damage after the fact."] },
  ],
}
);
